!! |> Section
!!     *Default signature.*
!!     For the sake of logical 
!!     uniformity 
!!     we assume that every function from $\rr$ to
!!     $\rr$ has a signature, where we can revert to
!!     ascribing the dimensionless signature 

!!     $$
!!     [1] \ra [1]
!!     $$

!!     for functions for which no other information is available.



    !! (See [Table 1.1](>>table_1_1), [Table 1.2](>>table_1_2),
    !! and the [discussion of differentiation](>>differentiation_and_signatures).)


    !! (For example, the differentiation operator produces
    !! a function of signature

    !! $$
    !! [\tA] \ra [\tB/\tA]
    !! $$

    !! when you pass it a function of signature

    !! $$
    !! [\tA] \ra [\tB]
    !! $$

    !! and so on for the other operators.)


!! |> Section
!!     *The differentation “operator”.*
!!     On a conceptual note,
!!     we can view differentiation
!!     as a function-that-takes-functions-and-outputs-functions,
!!     the “bigger fish” of the function world. As mathematicians
!!     use the term

!!     __operator__

!!     to denote a function that takes other functions as inputs,
!!     we therefore speak of the

!!     __differentiation operator__

!!     depicted here as an eye-of-Godzilla flying saucer: 

!!     |> Image
!!         src=images/c6_differentiation_operator_intro.svg

!!     !! (Generally speaking, will use such round-shaped function
!!     !! boxes to depict operators.) 
!!     !! (NB: We put the input tube of
!!     !! the differentiation operator on the left for no good reason,
!!     !! and will surely change our mind before long!)

!!     Note that the addition operation

!!     !! $$
!!     !! \Huge +
!!     !! $$
!!     |> Image
!!         src=images/c6_big_plus_symbol.svg

!!     on functions can also be understood as a kind of operator,
!!     albeit one with two inputs, since the inputs to the 
!!     operat
!!     |> del
!!         ion
!!     or are functions:

!!     |> Image
!!         src=images/c6_addition_operator_standalone.svg

!!     In this manner,
!!     all 
!!     operators that we have encountered so far in one form or another would include:

!!     |> Image
!!         src=images/c6_all_our_operators.svg

!!     Note that ‘$-$’ appears twice, once as a

!!     __binary__

!!     operator and once as a

!!     __unary__

!!     operator, meaning once as an operator that
!!     takes two inputs and once as an operator
!!     that takes one input, but these are really
!!     two different operators, that happen to share
!!     a symbol! (Same thing happens for the real
!!     numbers: there is a binary ‘$-$’ that you apply
!!     to an ordered pair of real numbers, and a
!!     unary ‘$-$’ that is applied to a single real
!!     number.) Likewise the differentation operator
!!     is

!!     __unary__

!!     since it takes only one function as input. 
!!     The effect of these operators on function signatures can
!!     also be given in pictorial form:
    
!!     |> Image
!!         src=images/c6_operators_acting_on_signatures.svg
    
